.zig-cache/
zig-out/

# The ARC *signing* vector, which the pre-receive hook will not accept: it carries
# ValiMail's published RSA test key inline, and gitleaks cannot tell a published vector
# from a live credential. Its sibling `arc-draft-validation-tests.yml` IS committed,
# because it has no key -- the asymmetry is deliberate, not an oversight.
#
# This is the one exception to the rule stated below, and it is bought back with a
# checksum: `test/arc_valimail/fetch-vectors.sh` pins the upstream commit and refuses
# any file whose SHA256 differs from the copy that used to be committed here. Pinned
# bytes verified on every run is a stronger guarantee than a vendored file that nobody
# re-checks. The key cannot be substituted with a generated one -- the fixture messages
# already contain sets signed by it. See that suite's README.
test/arc_valimail/arc-draft-sign-tests.yml

# Test *keys*, not the whole test directory. `test/` was ignored wholesale, which
# protected test/keys/arc.key -- a real private key -- but also silently excluded
# anything else placed under test/. That became a live problem when the RFC
# conformance harness needed to live there: a harness that is not committed is a
# claim rather than a test, and a .gitignore quietly dropping it is exactly how
# that happens without anyone noticing. Ignore the hazard, not its parent.
test/keys/

# Python bytecode from the conformance harness. Committed once already, in
# securespf, for want of this line.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The conformance harness virtualenv: thousands of third-party files, rebuilt from
# requirements.txt in one command. Noticed while committing the ARC signing suite,
# where `git status` offered to add the whole tree.
.venv/

# Crash dumps. FreeBSD writes <progname>.core into the cwd, so the name follows
# whichever binary died -- build.core, test.core, securearc.core. A single filename
# only ever blocks the crash that has already happened: build.core was listed here
# after one landed in a commit on 2026-07-26, and test.core walked straight past it
# on 2026-07-29 because the failing binary had a different name. Cores are tens of
# megabytes and can hold key material lifted from process memory, so match the
# pattern.
*.core
